Downloading and Installing PuTTY
-----------------------------------
PuTTY is a free SSH client for Windows. It connects you to a remote Linux server.

Download
----------
Go to the official site: https://www.chiark.greenend.org.uk/~sgtatham/putty/latest.html
Download the 64-bit MSI installer (putty-64bit-x.xx-installer.msi). It is the easiest option and includes PuTTY, PuTTYgen, and Pageant.

Install
--------
Double-click the .msi file and click Next through the wizard.
Keep the default install location (C:\Program Files\PuTTY).
Click Install, then Finish.

Connect to a Linux server
--------------------------
Open PuTTY from the Start menu.
Enter the server’s IP address in Host Name (or IP address).
Keep Port as 22 and Connection type as SSH.
Optionally type a name under Saved Sessions and click Save to reuse it.
Click Open. On the first connection, accept the security alert.
Enter your username and password at the prompt.

Connecting with a key file (e.g., AWS .pem)
--------------------------------------------

Open PuTTYgen, click Load, select your .pem file, then click Save private key to create a .ppk file.
In PuTTY, go to Connection → SSH → Auth → Credentials and browse to the .ppk file under “Private key file for authentication.”
Go back to Session and click Open. The username is usually ec2-user or ubuntu on AWS.
